{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And he said: Adonai, if now, etc.</b> To the greatest of them he said it, and he called all of them 'adonai,' and to the greatest he said: 'Do not pass by.' And since he would not pass by, his companions would stand with him — and in this formulation it is profane (Shevuot 35). Another interpretation: it is sacred — and he was saying to the Holy One, blessed be He, to wait for him until he would run and bring the guests in. And even though it is written afterward 'וַיָּרָץ לִקְרָאתָם,' the saying was before that. And the way of verses is to speak thus, as I explained by ״לֹא יָדוֹן רוּחִי בָאָדָם״ — which was written after ״וַיּוֹלֶד נֹחַ,״ yet it is impossible to say other than that the decree was twenty years before the birth. And both interpretations are in Bereshit Rabbah."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:59:08.466360112Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}